WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.283
So far, a broken computer has simply stopped, or come back with gaps in its memory.

2
00:00:08.283 --> 00:00:09.897
Now picture a worse one.

3
00:00:09.897 --> 00:00:11.915
It keeps talking, and it lies.

4
00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:18.008
Three computers decide one card payment, approve or decline, and one of them lies.

5
00:00:18.008 --> 00:00:20.003
Two honest against one liar.

6
00:00:20.003 --> 00:00:21.784
Surely the majority wins?

7
00:00:21.784 --> 00:00:22.924
Hold that guess.

8
00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:24.829
You've felt this at work.

9
00:00:24.829 --> 00:00:26.956
Your boss tells you: approve it.

10
00:00:27.233 --> 00:00:30.909
Then a colleague says: funny, the boss told me to decline.

11
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.347
Somebody is lying.

12
00:00:32.347 --> 00:00:34.514
Is it the boss, or your colleague?

13
00:00:34.514 --> 00:00:36.745
From where you sit, you can't tell.

14
00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:38.583
Computers do exactly this.

15
00:00:38.583 --> 00:00:43.743
One node, the leader, holds a verdict and sends it to the other two, the followers.

16
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.120
Each follower tells the other what the leader said.

17
00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.096
Then each one decides by majority over the two verdicts it heard.

18
00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:59.352
So the leader is the boss, the followers are you and your colleague, and passing on what the leader said is the chat by the coffee machine.

19
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.060
In world A, the leader is honest, and says approve.

20
00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.146
Follower 2 lies.

21
00:01:04.146 --> 00:01:07.471
It tells follower 1 that the leader said decline.

22
00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:12.488
So follower 1 hears approve from the leader, and decline from follower 2.

23
00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:14.705
In world B, the leader lies.

24
00:01:14.705 --> 00:01:18.305
It tells follower 1 approve, and follower 2 decline.

25
00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:21.796
Follower 2 is honest, and passes its decline on.

26
00:01:21.796 --> 00:01:26.708
So follower 1 hears approve from the leader, and decline from follower 2.

27
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.204
Exactly the same as in world A.

28
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.808
So what should follower 1 decide?

29
00:01:31.808 --> 00:01:32.927
Pause and guess.

30
00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:40.973
In world A, it must approve, because an honest leader's verdict has to win.

31
00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:48.527
In world B, it must decide the same as follower 2, the other honest node, whose view is the mirror of its own.

32
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:54.021
One verdict each way is a tie, and nothing in the tie tells the two worlds apart.

33
00:01:54.300 --> 00:02:00.131
Trust the leader on a tie, and world B splits: one follower approves, the other declines.

34
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:07.148
Pick a fixed answer instead, say decline, and in world A a lying follower overrules an honest leader.

35
00:02:07.433 --> 00:02:10.723
The lesson's lab tries every rule and every lie.

36
00:02:10.723 --> 00:02:13.190
With three nodes, every rule breaks.

37
00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:13.670
Well...

38
00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:16.548
two honest against one liar wasn't enough.

39
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.140
Add a fourth node.

40
00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:24.032
Now each follower hears three verdicts: one from the leader, and two passed on by the others.

41
00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:25.632
Let follower 2 lie.

42
00:02:25.632 --> 00:02:28.218
The leader says approve to everyone.

43
00:02:28.218 --> 00:02:33.247
Follower 2 tells follower 1 decline, and follower 3 passes on approve.

44
00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.121
The liar spoils one verdict out of three.

45
00:02:36.121 --> 00:02:40.404
Follower 1 still hears approve twice, and the honest majority wins.

46
00:02:40.667 --> 00:02:45.188
If the leader lies instead, the followers pass on honestly what they got.

47
00:02:45.188 --> 00:02:50.515
So every honest follower hears the same three verdicts, and reaches the same decision.

48
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:57.164
The lab runs every way one node can lie with four nodes, 40 rounds, and not one of them breaks.

49
00:02:57.533 --> 00:03:01.502
A node that lies like this has a name: a Byzantine fault.

50
00:03:01.502 --> 00:03:12.224
It comes from a 1982 paper by Lamport, Shostak and Pease, told as a story about generals agreeing on a plan by messenger, while some of them are traitors.

51
00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:17.772
The general rule: surviving f liars takes at least 3f + 1 nodes.

52
00:03:17.772 --> 00:03:18.679
Here's why.

53
00:03:18.967 --> 00:03:26.227
With N nodes, a decision can't wait for more than N minus f replies, because the faulty ones may never answer.

54
00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:29.031
Up to f of those replies may be lies.

55
00:03:29.031 --> 00:03:33.681
So at least N minus 2f are honest, and they must outnumber the lies.

56
00:03:33.933 --> 00:03:37.623
N minus 2f bigger than f means N bigger than 3f.

57
00:03:37.623 --> 00:03:39.621
For one liar, that's four.

58
00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:47.843
If computers only crash, 2f + 1 is enough, three for one crash, because a crashed node never says anything false.

59
00:03:48.233 --> 00:03:50.516
Some systems do pay for this.

60
00:03:50.516 --> 00:04:00.983
PBFT, short for practical Byzantine fault tolerance, runs 3f + 1 copies of a program, with three rounds of messages for each request.

61
00:04:01.233 --> 00:04:05.321
Its authors' file server ran only 3% slower than a plain one.

62
00:04:05.321 --> 00:04:11.487
Blockchains, shared records kept by parties who don't trust each other, use the same family.

63
00:04:11.733 --> 00:04:14.543
A payments database almost certainly doesn't.

64
00:04:14.543 --> 00:04:19.725
Surviving one liar takes four copies instead of three, and more rounds of messages.

65
00:04:19.967 --> 00:04:21.370
And it wouldn't help.

66
00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:25.981
Every copy runs the same code, so a bug lies the same way four times.

67
00:04:25.981 --> 00:04:30.525
Four copies of one bug just agree with each other, very confidently.

68
00:04:30.767 --> 00:04:35.727
An attacker's trick that breaks into one copy usually breaks into all of them.

69
00:04:35.727 --> 00:04:42.023
Checking who is asking, limiting what each program may touch, encryption and firewalls do that job.

70
00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:44.382
So, two honest against one liar?

71
00:04:44.382 --> 00:04:45.109
Not enough.

72
00:04:45.109 --> 00:04:50.991
A follower that hears one verdict each way can't tell a lying leader from a lying friend.

73
00:04:51.233 --> 00:04:54.928
Give it three verdicts, and one liar can only spoil one.

74
00:04:54.928 --> 00:04:56.644
One liar takes four nodes.

75
00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:58.204
One quick check.

76
00:04:58.204 --> 00:05:01.584
How many nodes does it take to survive two liars?

77
00:05:01.584 --> 00:05:02.688
Pause and think.

78
00:05:05.933 --> 00:05:06.480
Seven.

79
00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.848
Three times two, plus one.
